  "body": "The reading [c72f1efd24eb3946] claims the conjecture 'requires the two replies to be distinguished' and sets expect 'moves'. The conjecture says the opposite: 'the check, however, gives the same answer for both' and 'What would make me wrong: if recover_json_object returns {\"b\":2} for Reply A and {\"c\":3} for Reply B, or otherwise produces different outputs'. So the reading misrenders the conjecture: it expects a move where the conjecture predicts no move. The execution's 'unchanged, expected moves' is therefore a consequence of the reading's defect, not a failure of the conjecture. The rule's words 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text' are correctly read by the conjecture; the code's fenced pool captures the whole fence content as one candidate and falls through to top-level, so rule and code diverge as claimed.",
